---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Each week you get a deprecation notice for one real industry: the pitch
    for replacing it with AI, written the way that pitch is actually sold,
    followed by an audit of what the pitch leaves out. You read both halves,
    then write your own — a Weekly Audit Log entry naming one claim you can
    check, an original notice for an industry of your choosing, and, at the
    end of semester, a case defending or blocking a notice you didn't write
    in front of a review board.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who expects to be on the receiving end of an AI-replacement pitch
    at work, rather than writing one — or who wants to be able to tell the
    difference between a pitch's confident numbers and what they actually
    support. No AI or ML background is assumed; what's assumed is a
    willingness to read a persuasive paragraph twice.
  </p>

  <h2>How to read this course</h2>
  <p>
    Every lecture is written in two registers on purpose. The pitch and
    migration path are written the way the AI-consultancy pitch they
    describe was actually sold — confident, and light on cost. The breaking
    changes section is never written that way: it's the plain, checked half
    of the page, and it's where the cost the pitch left out actually shows
    up. If a page reads as overconfident, that's deliberate up to the point
    "Breaking changes" starts — past that seam, it's meant to read as plain
    fact-checking. The full version of this is on the
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
